----- stdout -----
Show a stored run's results again, without running anything.

aye keeps each run that ran something in `.aye/runs` (`[tool.aye] keep-runs`, 10 by default),
numbered `r1`, `r2`, … With no test named, the list of the last run: a line per result (each test
with its outcome, and for a failure its key and why; each failed lifetime, collection error or
module skipped while collecting), then the run's summary; paths alone filter it. Name tests to see
them in detail, as the run showed them: by the key the run printed (`aye show 3fae6`) or its start
(from 4 digits), test id or dotted name (a class's id shows its failed setup or teardown too), and
paths then select their tests too; `--failed` gives the details of everything that failed. Details
come at the second level unless `-v` or `-q` says otherwise; the flags mean what they mean in a run
(`aye show 3fae6 --output json` is what a coding agent wants; `-vv`, every frame, the output and the
locals). `--log` writes the run's raw events. A file of a test shown that changed since the run is
said first. The summary's exit code is the run's. Exit codes: 0 when it could show what was asked, 4
for a name no test of the run has (or the start of several keys), 5 when no run is stored.

Usage: aye show [OPTIONS] [TEST]...

Arguments:
  [TEST]...
          Test keys (`3fae6`) or their start, test ids, dotted names or paths

Options:
      --failed
          The details of everything that failed: tests that failed or errored, failed lifetimes,
          collection errors and failed workers

      --outcome <OUTCOME[,OUTCOME]>
          Only these outcomes: `passed`, `failed`, `error`, `skipped`, `expected_failure`,
          `unexpected_pass`

  -k <EXPR>
          Only tests matching the keyword expression, as the run's `-k`

  -m <EXPR>
          Only tests matching the tag expression, as the run's `-m`

      --run <RUN>
          An older stored run instead of the last: `r81` or `81` (`aye show --runs` lists them)

      --runs
          List the stored runs, newest first: run, start time, exit code and counts

      --log
          Write the run's log as stored, every event, one JSON object per line, whatever the output
          format

  -v, --verbose...
          More detail; repeat for more (up to -vvv). Without -v or -q, details come at -v in text,
          -vv in JSON

  -q, --quiet...
          Less: only the summary

      --output <FORMAT>
          Output format, as for a run

          Possible values:
          - text: For people: quiet on success, readable failures
          - json: JSON lines: the summary with -q, bad outcomes, failures in full with -v (for
            coding agents), every result with -vv, the raw event stream with -vvv

      --color <WHEN>
          When to use colour
          
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, always, never]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

